      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "(a) The endings of the future infinitives are the same as those of the present (and second aorist) infinitives: the difference lies solely in the stem.",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 4,
      "bbox": [
        138,
        194,
        153,
        903
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "(b) The meaning of the future infinitive is ‘to be about to –’, ‘to be going to –’.",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 5,
      "bbox": [
        159,
        194,
        193,
        915
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "(c) One common use of the future infinitive is with verbs that offer some future hope, intention or promise, for example:",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "box",
      "order": 6,
      "bbox": [
        209,
        93,
        334,
        915
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "• ἐλπίζω, ‘I hope/expect (to ...)’, e.g. ἐλπίζω νικήσειν ‘I hope/expect to win’\n• μέλλω, ‘I am about to, intend (to ...)’, e.g. ἔμελλε παύσεσθαι ‘she was about to stop’\n• ὑπισσχνέομαι, ‘I promise (to ...)’, e.g. ὑπισχνεῖται λήψεσθαι ‘he promises to take’",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": "•",
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
